Coastal wetlands, such as salt marshes and mangroves, act as natural buffers that significantly mitigate storm surge and wave energy. Their effectiveness stems from specific physical mechanisms rather than passive absorption. First, the dense vegetation and complex root systems increase surface roughness. As storm waves and surge waters move through this vegetation, friction slows the water flow, dissipating kinetic energy and reducing wave height. Second, wetlands induce wave breaking. In shallow, vegetated areas, waves become unstable and break earlier than they would over open water or hard surfaces, losing energy before reaching inland infrastructure. Third, wetlands provide vertical accretion